        "PrimaryFuelModel": "Brush (2 feet)",
        "ProjectedIncidentActivity12": "Fuels remain critically receptive. Localized showers from yesterday's pyrocumulous activity  late in the evening brought short-term relief overnight.  Hot and dry conditions combined with instability and fuel availability will drive fire spread. Potential for isolated afternoon T-storms in broader area this afternoon.",
        "ProjectedIncidentActivity24": "Fuels remain critically receptive. Hot and dry conditions will keep local diurnal winds active, and this combined with instability and fuel availability will drive fire spread. Decreased chance of isolated afternoon T-storms.",
        "ProjectedIncidentActivity48": "Fuels remain critically receptive. Hot and dry conditions will keep local diurnal winds active, and this combined with instability and fuel availability will drive fire spread. ",
        "ProjectedIncidentActivity72": "Fuels remain critically receptive. Hot and dry conditions will keep local diurnal winds active, and this combined with instability and fuel availability will drive fire spread. ",
        "ProjectedIncidentActivity72Plus": "Fuels remain critically receptive. Hot and dry conditions will keep local diurnal winds active, and this combined with instability and fuel availability will drive fire spread.",
        "ResidencesDestroyed": 5,
        "ResidencesThreatened": 8,
        "SecondaryFuelModel": "Closed Timber Litter",
        "SignificantEvents": "Observed fire behavior continued: running, flanking, group torching, short range spotting, smoldering. Conditions for active fire behavior and fire growth persist.  Active fire behavior remains driven  by fuel conditions, diurnal winds, and high instability. Timber/shrub fuel types most conducive for large fire growth.  ",
        "SummaryFuelModel": "Fuels in the fire area are broadly split between timber and grass types.  Timber models account for 45%, mostly Moderate Load Conifer Litter (TL3), and small, scattered areas of  TL8 (Long needle litter). Low Load, Dry Climate Grass (GS2) characterizes 27%.  Grass, shrub, and grass/shrub models comprise an additional 7%, timber/grass/shrub models comprise 17%. ",
        "TotalIncidentPersonnel": 1465,
        "UniqueFireIdentifier": "2026-UTMLF-005112",
        "WeatherConcerns": "Warm, unstable, and locally breezy conditions persist on Thursday. Minimum RH values bottom out around 9-13% across the fire area. Weakening mid-level moisture should help to reduce, but not quite end, chances (10-15%) for afternoon shower/thunderstorm activity. Any development in the afternoon (after 1300-1400) would be initiated over higher terrain features and isolated. Due to high instability any rapid plume growth could produce erratic surface inflows followed by strong and erratic outflow winds. General winds peak in the afternoon with westerly gusts up to 20-23 mph, especially for exposed ridgetop locations.\n",
