        "SummaryFuelModel": "Timber fuels remain dry and are still conducive to fire spread. Shrub and grass fuels have recovered with the higher humidities and won?t readily burn until less precipitation occurs and drier air settles in. ERCs across the forest are around the 60th percentile. 1000-hr fuels are at 10%.",
        "TotalIncidentPersonnel": 604,
        "UniqueFireIdentifier": "2026-UTMLF-005112",
        "WeatherConcerns": "Yesterday most of the fire area received at least 0.10-0.60 inches of rainfall due to showers and thunderstorms with more chances for activity through the week.  On Tuesday, after a dry start, expect showers and thunderstorm activity to begin after 1300-1400, peaking in likelihood during the late afternoon and evening hours.  The main concerns with this activity include lightning, gusty outflow winds, and heavy downpours.  Heavy rainfall over the burn-scar, especially where burn severity is great, may lead to localized flash flooding and/or debris flows - road have already been impacted by heavy rain this past week.  High temperatures rise into the 70s and 80s with minimum RHs in the 28-38% range.  Winds stay largely terrain driven, becoming variable and potentially erratic/gusty in the afternoon and evening due to outflow winds traversing the region.",
